From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] winebus: Show the PS5 title's other controllers as HID gamepads.

Patch 0471 gave XInput the other signed-in users' DualSenses, but
DirectInput, winmm, Raw Input and Windows.Gaming.Input find controllers
among winebus's devices, and the PS5 bus (patch 0951) had one.

The bus now makes a device for each of controllers 1..3 while the title
has it connected, with its own serial number, and removes it when the
controller goes: a game that counts controllers finds only players who
are there. Each device reads and rumbles its own controller. A device that
was asked to go is not replaced until it has stopped, since a new one
would have its instance ID. Controller 0's device stays always present.

The bus reads these controllers with pw_wine_pad_peek, which does not note
them as asked for: the bus runs in every game, and the title opens the
other users' pads only for a game that looks for controllers.

A game that looks through HID says so by opening a HID device. hidclass
now sends the minidriver IOCTL_HID_ACTIVATE_DEVICE, as Windows does, when a
device is first opened, and the bus passes that on to the title with
pw_wine_pads_ask. The title has no plugplay service, so the bus also asks
it to tell the game's windows (WM_DEVICECHANGE, patch 0984) some time after
a device comes or goes, once its interface has changed.

The devices are Xbox 360 controllers like controller 0's, so the HID stack
gives them the &IG_ gamepad interface as well: a game that reads both
XInput and HID finds each player once.

Programs list HID devices in the order of their instance IDs, and a game
such as Metal Slug 3 takes the first for its first player. hidclass now
reports its IDs as unique, so ntoskrnl no longer puts a hash of the parent
in front of them: the serial numbers decide, and controller 0 comes first.

A hosted game goes on once a gamepad's interface is up (patch 0952), which
could be before the others'. The title says how many are there at start in
WINE_PS5_HID_PADS, and ntoskrnl waits for that many, still within its
timeout: a game that looks for controllers once at start finds them all.
